  "script": "It was ten to six on a Saturday evening in late March, up on the east shore of Lake Tahoe. Ha-Leen-Ah decided she'd walk down to the water with Marley. Christian decided he'd ride. Everybody got there their own way.\n\nIt's about two miles down to the lake, and three hundred and fifty feet lower. He was there by six. And they were headed for one of their favorite places. Whale Beach.\n\n***\n\nHere's the wonderful thing about Whale Beach. The name is no joke. Out in the shallows sit great rounded boulders of granite, grey backs breaking the surface, and from the right angle you'd swear a pod of whales had just come up for air.\n\nIt was warm for March. Marley swam. And swam. And kept on swimming.\n\nAnd down at the water's edge, there was carnelian in the sand. Little pebbles of orange and amber, polished smooth by the lake, catching the low light like tiny lanterns.\n\n***\n\nAt seven, the drone went up for a look from above. Clear water, pale granite, the whales holding perfectly still. A few minutes later, the sun was sitting low over the mountains on the far shore, and there was a selfie, because of course there was.\n\nThen the climb back to the road. About four miles for the whole evening, and only about twenty-five minutes of it spent moving. The rest was time well spent.\n\n***\n\nLater, Christian took a photo of those rocks at sunset and asked an AI to imagine what the name suggests. In the clip, the boulders roll over, blow, and swim off into the gold.\n\nThe real ones are still there, of course. Still holding their breath. Waiting for the next visit.",
